# Docker Compose runtime configuration sample for the intranet MCP HTTP
# service.
#
# This sample contains only dummy, non-working placeholder values. Create your
# real runtime configuration file `.env.docker` from it (compose.yaml injects
# that file through `env_file` at start), replace every `replace-with...`
# placeholder with your deployment's real values, and keep `.env.docker` out
# of version control: it is git-ignored and docker-ignored, is only read at
# container start, and is never baked into the image.
# See "Docker Compose (direct TLS listener in the container)" in README.md.

# -- Required remote configuration -------------------------------------------
# Specific Microsoft Entra directory (tenant) GUID; "common" and domain names
# are refused at startup. Replace with your tenant's directory (tenant) ID.
ENTRA_TENANT_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# Confidential client (Application (client) ID) registered for this MCP
# deployment; holds no secret itself. Replace with your application ID.
ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111

# Client secret of the confidential client used for the on-behalf-of token
# exchange. Replace with your real client secret value (never commit it).
ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=replace-with-your-entra-client-secret

# -- Optional audience/scope overrides (defaults derive from ENTRA_CLIENT_ID) --
# With the Entra v2.0 endpoint the minimal setup derives all three of these
# from the client id above: the token audience defaults to the API app's
# client ID GUID, the required short scp scope defaults to mcp.access, and the
# upstream authorization request scope defaults to
# api://<ENTRA_CLIENT_ID>/<required short scope>. Set an override only when
# your deployment differs:
#
# Audience (aud claim) your issued tokens actually carry, when they differ
# from the client ID GUID default (for example a v1-style app ID URI).
#MCP_HTTP_AUDIENCE=11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111
#
# The short scope name(s) every token must carry in its scp claim when the
# derived one is not what your tokens carry. API-qualified api:// URIs are
# refused here; with MCP_HTTP_UPSTREAM_SCOPES set, the required scopes derive
# from that override's short scope part(s) instead of mcp.access.
#MCP_HTTP_REQUIRED_SCOPES=mcp.access
#
# Resource-qualified delegated scope(s) the upstream Microsoft Entra
# authorization request must use for your custom API (api://<app-id>/<scope>
# or https://<host>/<scope>). Set this explicitly when your app's Application
# ID URI differs from api://<ENTRA_CLIENT_ID> — no other URI shape is ever
# guessed; short form is never accepted here. In this single app setup it is
# the exposed API scope of the same application that owns
# ENTRA_CLIENT_ID.
#MCP_HTTP_UPSTREAM_SCOPES=api://11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111/mcp.access

# Every verified authenticated user may run the MCP tools. There is no separate
# mutation-group setting: tool authorization beyond delegated authentication is
# owned by the Entra enterprise-application assignment and the user's own
# Microsoft 365 permissions.

# Absolute https:// URL that MCP clients use to reach this deployment. The
# container binds a wildcard address (0.0.0.0), so there is no usable
# listener-derived default: set this to the externally reachable host name and
# port (for example https://<your-intranet-host>:8443) or the listener refuses
# to start. Required for the Compose deployment. This one exact URL (the
# "canonical" address) is what discovery, OAuth issuer metadata, and
# WWW-Authenticate challenges advertise; it is never derived from bind or Host
# header. For a local functional test it can be https://localhost:<port> (the
# default publish target is 127.0.0.1); the certificate must then carry
# "localhost" in its SANs and clients must trust it explicitly, for example
# with `curl --cacert tls/ca.pem ...` (never `--insecure`).
MCP_HTTP_EXTERNAL_BASE_URL=https://localhost:8443

# -- Optional remote configuration -------------------------------------------
# Comma-separated absolute https:// origins accepted from browser-based MCP
# clients (loopback origins stay allowed). Uncomment and adjust if needed.
#MCP_HTTP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://replace-with-your-intranet-client.example

# -- Authentication mode (persistent bridge chosen; direct optional) --------
# "bridge" (chosen, persistent): the standards OAuth sign-in flow below. The
# service holds each user's delegated upstream Entra credential, renews it
# with the tenant-pinned refresh grant (offline_access), keeps rotating
# refresh-token families for MCP clients in an encrypted durable store
# (bridge-store), and only a genuinely unusable upstream credential (the
# user repeating consent, or a revoked family) forces a fresh browser
# sign-in. Transient upstream or renewal outages answer 503 and retry.
# "entra" (direct mode, optional): MCP clients authorize directly against the
# tenant Entra authorization server (`https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant>/v2.0`
# advertised in discovery), using a pre-registered public client application
# with PKCE; the agent keeps its own refresh token client-side and the MCP
# server stores no user refresh tokens. Discovery then advertises only the
# Entra issuer and the fully resource-qualified request scopes, and token
# verification still requires the short scp scope name(s) configured above.
# No per-agent Entra application registration is required for the preferred
# bridge mode.
# Without MCP_HTTP_AUTH_MODE the mode is selected: bridge when
# MCP_HTTP_LOGIN_REDIRECT_URI is set, direct Entra otherwise.
#MCP_HTTP_AUTH_MODE=bridge

# -- Bridge sign-in for external MCP clients (the chosen mode) ---------------
# In direct "entra" mode the settings in this section are ignored. They apply
# to the bridge mode (automatic when the callback URL below is set, or forced
# with MCP_HTTP_AUTH_MODE=bridge).
# Configure the exact HTTPS callback URL registered for this service's browser
# sign-in (Web platform redirect URI of the MCP service app, ending in
# /login/callback). Setting this variable enables the standards-based MCP
# authorization flow described in README.md. Register it in Entra as a Web
# platform redirect URI on the same confidential app that owns
# ENTRA_CLIENT_ID/ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET and exposes the delegated API scope
# (MCP_HTTP_REQUIRED_SCOPES and MCP_HTTP_UPSTREAM_SCOPES; see the derived
# defaults above). For a local
# test this is the canonical base URL plus /login/callback (for example
# https://localhost:8443/login/callback) and must match the registered Entra
# redirect exactly.
#MCP_HTTP_LOGIN_REDIRECT_URI=https://localhost:8443/login/callback

# Durable, encrypted bridge credential store directory: MCP refresh-token
# families, delegated identities, and the server-held upstream Entra refresh
# credentials (all AES-256-GCM encrypted at rest). The directory must be
# mounted on a durable volume and used by exactly one service replica; the
# default in container deployments is /app/data/oauth (the Compose mount of
# T20). Without this variable the CLI runtime defaults to
# ~/.outlook-mcp/remote-oauth, which is NOT durable in a container until it
# is mounted. Store it and its key together in your protected backups.
#MCP_HTTP_BRIDGE_STORE_DIR=/app/data/oauth
#
# Optional separate (already-mounted, read-only-to-the-service) key file with
# exactly 32 key bytes for the bridge store; the default is a private
# auto-generated key inside the store directory. The key and the encrypted
# store files must be backed up together and never share a file.
#MCP_HTTP_BRIDGE_KEY_FILE=/app/secrets/bridge-store.key

# Pre-registered external MCP clients as a JSON array. Every client carries
# its exact redirect URI(s) — that client's own browser callback address where
# the authorization code and state are returned — registered once here and
# exactly; use clientSecret only for confidential clients (public clients use
# PKCE). Never place Entra secrets here. Unregistered clients and redirects are
# refused; dynamic registration is off by default (see below). Per-client
# OAuth capability was not verified against real MCP clients.
#MCP_HTTP_OAUTH_CLIENTS=[{"clientId":"external-mcp-client","clientSecret":"replace-for-confidential-clients-only","redirectUris":["https://replace-with-client-callback.example/oauth/callback"]}]

# MCP scope(s) external clients may request (defaults to "mcp"; spaces or
# commas separate several scopes).
#MCP_HTTP_OAUTH_SUPPORTED_SCOPES=mcp

# Opt-in dynamic client registration (default off; prefer pre-registration).
#MCP_HTTP_OAUTH_ALLOW_DYNAMIC_REGISTRATION=false

# -- Local development (compose.local.yaml), HTTP without TLS ---------------
# For explicitly opted-in LOCAL testing, `compose.local.yaml` (local
# development only; NOT for intranet or production) overrides the two
# HTTPS keys above to their plaintext loopback equivalents internally:
# MCP_HTTP_EXTERNAL_BASE_URL=http://localhost:8443 and
# MCP_HTTP_LOGIN_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:8443/login/callback, and it
# sets MCP_HTTP_ALLOW_LOCAL_HTTP=true itself. Do not set these overrides
# here: your production-shaped values above stay untouched, and nothing
# needs to change in your environment for local runs. Register the loopback
# HTTP callback `http://localhost:8443/login/callback` in Entra for the
# local flow (Microsoft permits plain HTTP redirect URIs only for loopback
# addresses).
#
# -- Optional production image override (compose.yaml only) -------------------
# compose.yaml defaults its service image to the locally built
# outlook-mcp-http:local tag (built with --build, or loaded from the release
# archive; see README.md). To run the published container image instead,
# override MCP_HTTP_IMAGE through the shell environment, the Compose project
# .env file, or an explicit --env-file, for example:
#
#   MCP_HTTP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/actsis/outlook-mcp:<version> \
#     docker compose -f compose.yaml up -d --no-build --pull always
#
#MCP_HTTP_IMAGE=ghcr.io/<owner>/<repository>:<tag>
#
# Compose interpolation of ${MCP_HTTP_IMAGE} is resolved by Compose itself
# from those sources only. The service-level env_file (.env.docker) does NOT participate
# in Compose ${...} substitution and cannot set this variable
# before the image is chosen; never rely on .env.docker injecting the image.

# -- Not applicable here -----------------------------------------------------
# USE_TEST_MODE is a local-only setting and must stay unset (or false):
# the HTTP mode refuses to start when USE_TEST_MODE=true.
# TLS certificate paths are provided by compose.yaml (MCP_HTTP_TLS_CERT /
# MCP_HTTP_TLS_KEY point at the read-only mount /run/tls); never put private
# keys in this file.
